arXiv:submit/4885373
From: Ajax Benander <akb9408@rit.edu>
Date: Tue, 23 May 2023 13:47:40 EST   (14kb)

Title: Human Existence Logically Implies Past Dinosaur Existence
Authors: Ajax Benander
Categories: q-bio.OT stat.AP
Comments: 8 pages
License: http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
\\
  Dinosaurs are often perceived in a light of symbolizing the most ancient
corners of life on Earth through their immortal fossils that they leave behind.
Through this, we have a natural tendency to regard them as somewhat irrelevant
to the modern day, and intangible for it. However, due to new research
involving the fundamental power of Bayes' theorem, it may be concluded that
dinosaurs had a surprising impact on the enabling of human evolution throughout
the expanses of natural history. As a matter of fact, this impact may
potentially be so exaggerated, that the very state of human life in the present
logically implies that dinosaurs at some point walked the Earth. To be clear,
we obviously are well aware that dinosaurs once existed, but now with this
logic, we can further show that they were a past necessity to the eventual
appearance of the human mind, up-leveling their importance relative to us to
some form of cruciality.
\\

