Dear Ajax Benander,

Thank you for your email and your interest in submitting your manuscript 
to Quantum. As you are probably aware, posting a paper on the arXiv 
prior to submission is mandatory I am afraid. Since it's a definite 
requirement to do so as per journal's policy 
<https://quantum-journal.org/editorial-policies/>, we cannot really make 
exceptions here, unfortunately.

We do hope that you will succeed in finding an endorser after all and 
wish you all the best for this endeavour. Once a submission to arXiv has 
been completed, subsequent submission to Quantum is of course possible 
and welcome any time.

Sorry we cannot share better news for now.

Best regards,

Lukas (admin)


Am 19.03.2025 um 02:40 schrieb Ajax Benander (RIT Student):
> Dear Quantum Editorial Team,
>
> I hope this email finds you well. I am writing to inquire about the 
> possibility of submitting my manuscript, "A Condensed Overview of the 
> Aethic Solution to the Measurement Problem," despite not having an 
> arXiv preprint. As an undergraduate researcher, I currently do not 
> have direct access to post on arXiv, but my manuscript is publicly 
> available on PhilArchive <https://philarchive.org/rec/BENARA-5> (full 
> paper) and ResearchGate 
> <https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387198187_A_Condensed_Overview_of_the_Aethic_Solution_to_the_Measurement_Problem> (condensed version).
>
> Of course, I should probably state the idea of the paper itself: the 
> premise is simply a couple of philosophical considerations for how to 
> craft a totally perspectivist view of quantum measurement, 
> specifically where superpositions themselves are derived from a more 
> general inductive step as is integrated with two other axioms into 
> larger logical framework. At its heart this is a fundamentally 
> interpretive idea rather than a theory or anything like that, but some 
> of the logical implications that come out of it, (namely concerning 
> the double-slit experiment), are interesting enough that I'm dying to 
> share it with people. I don't have an endorser, however, because it 
> has been challenging to meet anybody at the intersection of 
> philosophy, physics, and mathematical logic altogether. As such, I'm 
> in a bit of a predicament.
>
> Would it be possible to submit my manuscript directly to Quantum under 
> these circumstances? If so, I would greatly appreciate any guidance on 
> how best to proceed.
>
> Thank you for your time and consideration. I look forward to your 
> response.
>
> Best regards,
> Ajax Benander
> Undergraduate Student
> Rochester Institute of Technology
> akb9408@rit.edu
