        <div dir="ltr"><span class="gmail-im" style="color:rgb(80,0,80)">Dear
            Quantum Editorial Team,<br>
            <br>
            I hope this email finds you well. I am writing to inquire
            about the possibility of submitting my manuscript, "A
            Condensed Overview of the Aethic Solution to the Measurement
            Problem," despite not having an arXiv preprint. As an
            undergraduate researcher, I currently do not have direct
            access to post on arXiv, but my manuscript is publicly
            available on <a href="https://philarchive.org/rec/BENARA-5"
              target="_blank" moz-do-not-send="true">PhilArchive</a> (full
            paper) and <a
href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387198187_A_Condensed_Overview_of_the_Aethic_Solution_to_the_Measurement_Problem"
              target="_blank" moz-do-not-send="true">ResearchGate</a> (condensed version).<br>
            <br>
          </span>Of course, I should probably state the idea of the
          paper itself: the premise is simply a couple of philosophical
          considerations for how to craft a totally perspectivist view
          of quantum measurement, specifically where superpositions
          themselves are derived from a more general inductive step as
          is integrated with two other axioms into larger logical
          framework. At its heart this is a fundamentally interpretive
          idea rather than a theory or anything like that, but some of
          the logical implications that come out of it, (namely
          concerning the double-slit experiment), are interesting enough
          that I'm dying to share it with people. I don't have an
          endorser, however, because it has been challenging to meet
          anybody at the intersection of philosophy, physics, and
          mathematical logic altogether. As such, I'm in a bit of a
          predicament. <span class="gmail-im" style="color:rgb(80,0,80)">
            <div><br>
              Would it be possible to submit my manuscript directly to
              Quantum under these circumstances? If so, I would greatly
              appreciate any guidance on how best to proceed.<br>
              <br>
